  <title>7.16.2: Graph Functions in Vertex Form</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02027</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.16.2: Graph Functions in Vertex Form</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d6e0c3b4-90cd-49fc-8895-a52dcb346428</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>Examine these two equations that define quadratic functions.</para>
<para>\(p(x)= -(x−4)^2+10\)</para>
<para>\(q(x)=\frac {1}{2}(x−4)^2+10\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>The graph of \(p\) passes through \((0,-6)\) and \((4,10)\), as shown on the coordinate plane.</item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/70b01f6147ee71f3375b8b75b04e230921ad040c"/></media></figure></para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Find the coordinates of another point on the graph of \(p\). Explain or show your reasoning. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\((8,-6)\). If we find \(p(8)\) and substitute 8 for \(x\) in the expression, we have:
      \(p(8)=-(8−4)^2+10\), which is equal to \(-16+10\) or -6.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Plot \(p(x)= -(x−4)^2+10\) and the points \((0,
    -6)\), \((4, 10)\), and \((8, -6)\) to label the graph. </item>
</list>
<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
  
    <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para><figure><media alt="GRAPH OF A DOWNWARD PARABOLA WITH A \(y\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 6, A VERTEX AT THE POINT (4, 10), AND PASSING THROUGH THE POINTS (2, 6) AND (6, 6)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f1115944b5a4693784d28b587b77e28a2b9bf5ad"/></media></figure></para>
  <para>The graph of a quadratic function is symmetrical across the vertical line containing the vertex. If a point on the
    graph that is 4 units to the left of the line containing the vertex has a \(y\)-value of -6, there is a point to the
    right of the line containing the vertex that also has a \(y\)-value of -6.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<para>A line containing the vertex is called the parabola’s <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">axis of symmetry</term>.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the \(x\)- and \(y\)-coordinates of the vertex and sketch the graph.</item>
</list>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="a34b1eee-8c54-4cca-a589-4a07609ff811" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="0" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--Q1a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="78e6d386-4940-4f6a-8ef2-6059b0a86223" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="4">
    <problem>
      <para> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of \(q(x)=\frac {1}{2}(x−4)^2+10\)? </para>
      <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 4. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex is found by using the squared term of the vertex form. The
        \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the function \(q\) is 4.</para>
    </solution>
        </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q1b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="571dad64-a507-4ea7-bb6b-bf773bf7ad4b" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="10">
    <problem>
      <para> What is the the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of \(q(x)=\frac {1}{2}(x−4)^2+10\)? </para>
      <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 10. In vertex form, the \(y\)-coordinate is the constant term. The value of the \(y\)-coordinate of
        the vertex of the function \(q\) is 10.</para>
    </solution>
        </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Do not edit below line.-->
  <solution>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </div>
</div>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. On the same coordinate plane containing the points
        and equation from question 1, plot the coordinates of the vertex of the function \(q\). </para>
    <para>Select the <emphasis>Solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para><figure><media alt="GRAPH OF A DOWNWARD PARABOLA WITH A \(y\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 6, A VERTEX AT THE POINT (4, 10), AND PASSING THROUGH THE POINTS (2, 6) AND (6, 6)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/3313f94d34630ae5e05743629e176ab8ea73d7d8"/></media></figure></para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> Find two other points that are on the graph of \(q\). Try to identify points that are symmetrical over the
        axis of symmetry. </para>
    <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <list>
    <item> One possible answer could be \((2,12)\) and \((6,12)\). Substituting 2 for \(x\), we have: \(q(2)=\frac
      {1}{2}(2−4)^2+10\), which is 12, so \((2,12)\) is one point on the graph. Substituting 6 for \(x\), we have:
      \(q(6)=\frac {1}{2} 1(6−4)^2+10\), which equals 12, so \((6,12)\) is another point. </item>
  </list>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent">
    <item> Another possible answer could be \((0,18)\) and \((8,18)\). Substituting 0 for \(x\), we get \(q(0)=18\), so
      \((0,18)\) is one point on the graph. This point is 4 units to the left and 8 units up from the vertex. Because
      the graph has a vertical line of symmetry, or axis of symmetry, through the vertex, it also goes through a point
      that is 4 units to the right and 8 units up, which is \((8,18)\). </item>
  </list>
</solution></exercise>
</example>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Sketch and label the graph of \(q\). Explain or show your
    reasoning. </item>
</list>
<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
  
    <para>Select the <emphasis>Solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e5fbb242593d9cc5cbf800151cadcf53bc7e57ff"/></media></figure></para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Recall that \(p(x)= -(x−4)^2+10\). Pilar says, "Once I know the vertex is \((4,10)\), I can find out,
    without graphing, whether the vertex is the maximum or the minimum of function \(p\). I would just compare the
    coordinates of the vertex with the coordinates of a point on either side of it."</item>
</list>
<para>Complete the table by answering questions a and b.</para>
<table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
  <thead/>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">
        \(x\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(3\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(4\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(5\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">
        \(p(x)\)
      </entry>
      <entry> </entry>
      <entry>
        \(10\)
      </entry>
      <entry> </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="6e40f487-861c-44ea-b3c1-50a99d4d6075" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="0" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--Q1a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="92d7e8c5-74c3-4477-80d7-86504f822391" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="9">
    <problem>
      <para> Evaluate \(p(3)\). </para>
      <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 9. \(p(3)=-(3−4)^2+10=-(-1)^2+10=-1+10=9\).</para>
    </solution>
        </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q1b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="ea4be6a1-ae97-4d9c-b1c5-552538426d88" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="9">
    <problem>
      <para> Evaluate \(p(5)\). </para>
      <para>Enter your answer here: </para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 9. \(p(5)=-(5−4)^2+10=-(1)^2+10=-1+10=9\).</para>
    </solution>
        </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Do not edit below line.-->
  <solution>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </div>
</div>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Explain how Pilar might have reasoned whether the vertex is the minimum or maximum. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The values of \(p(3)\) and \(p(5)\) are both less than 10. The vertex of a quadratic graph represents the minimum
      or the maximum of the function. Because the two points on either side of the vertex of \(p\) have a lesser
      \(y\)-value, the vertex must be the maximum.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

