<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="e55bed11-0d84-4dd0-bc91-838637f4b9a6" data-retry-limit="0" data-schema-version="1.0" data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <exercise data-content-id="05de72a4-b09c-456c-8864-85a6af2836ca" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-solution="\(y=-2(x-2)^2+3\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(y=-2(x-2)^2+3\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=-3(x-2)^2+2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=-5(x-2)^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=2(x-2)^2+3\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
      <para>Choose the equation for a quadratic function whose graph has the vertex at \((2,3)\) and contains the point
          \((0,-5)\).</para>
      </problem>
  </exercise>
  <solution>
    <para>Correct! \(y=-2(x-2)^2+3\) To have a vertex at \((2,3)\), the equation in vertex form is \(y=a(x-2)2+3\) where a
      is some number. When \(x=0\) this equation takes the value \(4a+3\). To pass through \((0,-5)\) we need
      \(4a+3=-5\) so \(a=-2\). So the equation is \(y=-2(x-2)^2+3\).</para>
  </solution>
  </div>
